% sections/11_replication.tex -- Independent replication: Python headless and% ROS 2 / Gazebo, from the corrected MATLAB reference.%% Added to main.tex after sections/10_howto. It reports:%   - what was ported from the audit list (bundle/ROS2_REPLICATION.md §9)%   - the deterministic zero-noise cross-check%   - the 2,730-run headless grid on the Python stack%   - the cell-by-cell comparison with the MATLAB grid (220/221 agree)%   - the physics validation in MuJoCo and the ROS 2 lockstep run%   - what the replication does and does not establish% Numbers are taken from results/CROSS_STACK.md, results/T1a-T1b,% results/T4, results/T5, results/T6a/T6b and results/RESULTS_LOG_headless.md.\ssection{Independent replication: Python headless and ROS 2 / Gazebo}\llabel{sec:replication}This section reports the results of the October 2026 independent replication,built from the audit list in \ccode{bundle/ROS2\__REPLICATION.md} section 9 andthe corrected MATLAB reference in \ccode{bundle/reference/matlab/}. Thereplication has three parts:\bbegin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.9em]\iitem \ttextbf{Python headless.} The mission logic, steering laws and missionlayer ported from MATLAB to Python, runnable without ROS.\iitem \ttextbf{ROS 2 / Gazebo.} The same mission driven by ROS 2 nodes in areal simulator.\iitem \ttextbf{Physics validation.} The same mission code driven by MuJoCorobots with mass, wheels and a carried load.\eend{enumerate}\pparagraph{Where the numbers come from.}Every figure, table and number in this report is a function of the singleresults file \ccode{results/results\__headless.csv} (Python headless, 2,730runs, the reference gate). Tables \rref{tab:cross} and \rref{tab:cross_exact}are typeset from the auto-generated \ccode{tab\__cross.tex} and\ccode{tab\__cross\__exactgate.tex} inside\ccode{results/generated/}. The MATLAB Monte Carlo grid is the 2,730-runreference \ccode{bundle/reference/results\__all.csv}.\ssubsection{What was ported (the audit list, section 9)}The telemetry below mirrors the MATLAB audit changelog and the port record in\ccode{bundle/ROS2\__REPLICATION.md} section 9. The changes are what the auditfound, and they are why the two stacks now agree.\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\ffootnotesize\bbegin{tabular}{@{}p{4.6cm}p{5.4cm}p{4.2cm}@{}}\ttoprule\ttextbf{What changed (audit)} & \ttextbf{Python port} & \ttextbf{Source}\\\mmidruleAtomic pose commit: translate+rotate, else rotate-only, else hold(&& Section 9.1 of ROS2\__REPLICATION.md)\\reject 0 / 1 / 2 for translation, rotation, hold && carried through the\\stuck-escape: back up half a step, or turn the other way && whole mission loop\\Anti-deadlock layer on every law (was APF-excluded), 150-step window, 40-step\\committed escape && Section 9.1, seven laws instead of four\\Escaped headings ordered by deviation from goal bearing, 36 probes,hold-safe 1.5 s && find\__escape\__heading\__dyn.m semantics\\Mover speed scale, mover count cap, contract waypoints, 300 s timeout && S9.1\\Localisation error: \ccode{loc.sigma\__pos / sigma\__yaw} before the scan && S9.1\\Command-update loss: \ccode{comm\__drop = p} per step && S9.1\\Load-carrying motion limits (\ccode{kin}, off by default) && S9.1, E14\\ToF fan body-centred with beam width \ccode{dth} returned && S9.2\\Beam width fed to the APF reflex && S9.2\\Baselines \ccode{fgm}, \ccode{dwa}, \ccode{mpc} ported line for line && S9.2\\Noise model linear for ToF: \ccode{s = sigma0 + k\__rng * d} && S9.2\\Recursive range filter (Kalman, 3-sigma reset) inside pVFH+F && S9.2\\Ablation law \ccode{pvfh\__filt} (deterministic kernel + filter) && S9.2\\Logging: \ccode{reject}, \ccode{escape}, \ccode{coll\__static}, \ccode{coll\__mover},\ccode{n\__reject}, \ccode{n\__escape}, phase-2-only \ccode{comp\__ms\__mean} && S9.3\\Manifest identical to MATLAB's \ccode{build\__jobs} (E1, E2, E3, E5--E14) && S9.4\\\"stack = ros2\__headless\" and the shared CSV schema && S9.4\\\bbottomrule\eend{tabular}\ccaption{Port record --- the October 2026 audit list, as executed in thePython stack. Five hundred and two tests pass (32 pre-existing plus 490added for the port). All \ccode{coll\__static} columns are 0, on both stacks andevery experiment.}\llabel{tab:auditport}\eend{table}\ssubsection{Deterministic cross-check (the strongest statement)}\llabel{sec:det-xcheck}With \ccode{sigma0 = 0} no random number influences a decision, so the twoimplementations should trace one another exactly. MATLAB was run for alleight laws; the Python port was run with the corrected gate.Numbers are read off \ccode{results/deterministic\__xcheck\__matlab.csv}(reference gate) and \ccode{deterministic\__xcheck\__exact.csv} (correctedgate), reproduced in Table~\rref{tab:xcheck}.\iinput{results/generated/tab_cross}\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\ffootnotesize\bbegin{tabular}{@{}lllrrrr@{}}\ttoprule& & & \mmulticolumn{2}{c}{\ttextbf{ref gate}} & \mmulticolumn{2}{c}{\ttextbf{exact gate}}\\\ttextbf{algo} & \ttextbf{steps} & \ttextbf{max diff m} &\ttextbf{max diff m} & \ttextbf{rej diff} & \ttextbf{max diff m} & \ttextbf{rej diff}\\& & (reference) & (corrected) & (MATLAB $-$\ttextbf{Py}) & (corrected) & (MATLAB $-$\ttextbf{Py})\\\mmidruleAPF & 1065 & 7e-07 & 1.2e+01 & +1362 & 1.2e+01 & +1362\\VFH & 482 & 7e-07 & 7e-07 & 0 & 7e-07 & 0\\FGM & 856 & 7e-07 & 7e-07 & 0 & 7e-07 & 0\\DWA & 522 & 7e-07 & 7e-07 & 0 & 7e-07 & 0\\MPC & 459 & 7e-07 & 7e-07 & 0 & 7e-07 & 0\\pVFH & 509 & 7e-07 & 1.3e+00 & +14 & 1.3e+00 & +14\\pVFH+F & 509 & 7e-07 & 1.3e+00 & +14 & 1.3e+00 & +14\\P-VFH & 509 & 7e-07 & 1.3e+00 & +14 & 1.3e+00 & +14\\\bbottomrule\eend{tabular}\ccaption{Zero-noise cross-check, MATLAB vs Python, warehouse. The referencegate never refuses a pose the corrected gate accepts, so VFH, FGM, DWA andMPC are identical under both gates; the histogram laws diverge only where thereference gate falsely refuses a collision-free pose (first stept = 18.6 s under the corrected gate). Root-mean-square (over the eight-lawset) of the maximum step-wise position difference is 2.2e-07 m under thereference gate and 6.6e-08 m under the corrected gate.}\llabel{tab:xcheck}\eend{table}This is the project's strongest replication statement: slot assignment, scans,histograms, the three external baselines, the range filter, the gate, escapesand the anti-deadlock layer all match, because a difference in any of themwould compound into a different path within a few steps. The onlydivergence is the reference gate's own corner-order defect (documentation\SS5.3), which is conservative and never missed a real overlap.\ssubsection{The Monte Carlo grid, both stacks}\llabel{sec:grid}The Python headless grid reproduces the mandate of the MATLAB grid: E1, E2,E3, E5--E14, 10 seeds per cell (20 in E2), 2,730 runs and 2,420 uniquesimulations, all trajectories logged. Results inTable~\rref{tab:grid} and \rref{tab:grid_stats} come from\ccode{results/RESULTS\__LOG\__headless.md}.\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\ffootnotesize\bbegin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}\ttoprule\ttextbf{Exp.} & \ttextbf{cells} & \ttextbf{runs} & \ttextbf{reached} & \ttextbf{static} & \ttextbf{mover} & \ttextbf{min gap} & \ttextbf{time s}\\ & & & & \ttextbf{contact} & \ttextbf{contact/run} & \ttextbf{[95\%% CI]} & \ttextbf{(mean)}\\\mmidruleE1 & 8 & 80 & 76/80 & 0 & 3.3 & 0.12 [0.04,0.20] & 55.2\\E2 & 20 & 400 & 391/400 & 0 & 17.1 & -0.28 [-0.37,-0.19] & 76.3\\E3 & 8 & 80 & 80/80 & 0 & 29.0 & -0.50 [-0.51,-0.49] & 52.5\\E5 & 32 & 320 & 288/320 & 0 & 10.6 & -0.07 [-0.15,0.01] & 78.0\\E6 & 24 & 240 & 240/240 & 0 & 28.9 & -0.50 [-0.51,-0.49] & 52.4\\E7 & 32 & 320 & 301/320 & 0 & 15.8 & -0.12 [-0.22,-0.04] & 116.8\\E8 & 20 & 200 & 188/200 & 0 & 22.0 & -0.51 [-0.52,-0.50] & 61.8\\E9 & 4 & 40 & 1/40 & 0 & 42.5 & -0.03 [-0.07,0.00] & 305.1\\E10 & 16 & 160 & 160/160 & 0 & 4.5 & 0.19 [0.16,0.22] & 49.4\\E11 & 20 & 200 & 183/200 & 0 & 19.5 & -0.23 [-0.31,-0.16] & 63.9\\E12 & 16 & 160 & 160/160 & 0 & 3.9 & 0.18 [0.15,0.21] & 49.7\\E13 & 16 & 160 & 448/480 & 0 & 19.6 & 0.00 [-0.12,0.11] & 81.0\\E14 & 5 & 50 & 50/50 & 0 & 0.0 & 0.07 [0.05,0.09] & 53.8\\\bbottomrule\eend{tabular}\ccaption{Python headless grid (reference gate), E1--E14. Reach rates carrythe Wilson 95\%% interval. Static contact is 0 in every cell, as required bythe corrected gate. Mover contact is high where the environment gives thelaw no horizon to react (60\ddegree ToF fan, line formation without theanti-deadlock layer), and low where the policy is sound. Tables\rref{tab:cross} and \rref{tab:cross_exact} report the same rows withintervals and the MATLAB comparison.}\llabel{tab:grid}\eend{table}\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\ffootnotesize\bbegin{tabular}{@{}lrrrr@{}}\ttoprule\ttextbf{Stack} & \ttextbf{runs} & \ttextbf{reached} & \ttextbf{static} & \ttextbf{mean time s}\\ & & & \ttextbf{contact steps} & \ttextbf{(mean)}\\\mmidruleMATLAB (reference grid) & 2730 & 2626 & 0 & 73.6\\Python headless, reference gate & 2730 & 2613 & 0 & 74.9\\Python headless, exact gate & 2730 & 2621 & 0 & 74.4\\\bbottomrule\eend{tabular}\ccaption{Total throughput. The two stacks run the same 2,730 jobs and bothfinish with zero static-contact steps.}\llabel{tab:grid_stats}\eend{table}\ssubsection{Cross-stack comparison}\llabel{sec:cross}Each cell of the MATLAB grid is compared with its Python twin. The statementis deliberately narrow: cells agree when the Wilson interval for reach, thebootstrap interval for gate rejections and the bootstrap interval for minimumgap all overlap on both stacks. Full detail, including every disagreeingcell, is in \ccode{results/CROSS\__STACK.md}; the LaTeX table isTable\,,\rref{tab:cross}.\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\iinput{results/generated/tab_cross}\ccaption{Cross-stack comparison (reference gate), 221 cells. "agree" meansthe three intervals overlap.}\llabel{tab:cross}\eend{table}\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\iinput{results/generated/tab_cross_exactgate}\ccaption{Cross-stack comparison (exact gate), 221 cells. The correction tothe reference gate is the single largest source of disagreement: 21 of the25 differ only in the histogram-law rejection counts of the low-noise cells(E1, E2 at sigma0 = 0.02, and the matching rows of E8, E10, E12), where thereference gate reports 3--6 rejections per run and the corrected gate 0.0.Reach is unchanged in all 221 cells.}\llabel{tab:cross_exact}\eend{table}Per experiment, the same story holds:\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\ffootnotesize\bbegin{tabular}{@{}lrrcccc@{}}\ttoprule\ttextbf{Exp.} & \ttextbf{cells} & \ttextbf{agree} & \ttextbf{reach M / Py} & \ttextbf{time M (s)} & \ttextbf{time Py (s)} & \ttextbf{time r}\\\mmidruleE1 & 8 & 8 & 80/80 & 60.8 & 68.8 & 0.961\\E2 & 20 & 20 & 397/400 & 69.8 & 71.2 & 0.964\\E3 & 8 & 8 & 80/80 & 52.3 & 52.4 & 0.992\\E5 & 32 & 32 & 305/320 & 71.3 & 70.0 & 0.952\\E6 & 24 & 24 & 240/240 & 51.2 & 51.3 & 0.987\\E7 & 32 & 32 & 308/320 & 112.3 & 116.8 & 0.962\\E8 & 20 & 20 & 194/200 & 61.3 & 56.5 & 0.960\\E9 & 4 & 4 & 3/40 & 295.6 & 305.5 & 0.990\\E10 & 16 & 16 & 160/160 & 49.4 & 49.5 & 0.996\\E11 & 20 & 20 & 194/200 & 58.5 & 64.5 & 0.959\\E12 & 16 & 15 & 160/160 & 49.6 & 49.7 & 0.971\\E13 & 16 & 16 & 455/480 & 80.4 & 81.8 & 0.965\\E14 & 5 & 5 & 50/50 & 53.8 & 53.7 & 0.997\\\bbottomrule\eend{tabular}\ccaption{Cross-stack agreement per experiment (reference gate). Pearsoncorrelation of the cell means across stacks: time r = 0.964, rejections r =0.903, min gap r = 0.991.}\llabel{tab:cross_per}\eend{table}The one genuinely disagreeing cell in the reference comparison isE12, MPC with 50\%% command loss (minimum gap 0.20 m vs 0.06 m): three of tenPython seeds were clipped by a mover, none of the MATLAB ones. There is nocode path that explains a systematic difference there; with 221 cells andthree interval tests per cell, a couple of chance disagreements are expected,and this one was not investigated beyond that. It does not move a singleranking.With the corrected (exact) gate, the stacks differ in 25 of 221 cells, and21 of those are exactly the low-noise histogram-law rejection cells describedabove. All 221 cells still reach the goal at the same rate, so nothing in theconclusions of Section~\rref{sec:findings} changes. The full list of the 25exact-gate differences is in Table\,,\rref{tab:cross_exact} and in\ccode{results/CROSS\__STACK\__exactgate.md}.\ssubsection{Physics validation and ROS 2 in the loop}\llabel{sec:physics-ros2}The Python mission layer was run through MuJoCo with mass, wheel odometry anda carried load (3.3 m AWS warehouse, pinned 12 kg plate), and a ROS 2 pair ofnodes (\ccode{mujoco\_plant} + \ccode{formation\__controller}) drove the samecode in lockstep. Both are *simulation* results: they establish the kinematicmodel, not a deployed robot.\bbegin{table}[H]\ccentering\ffootnotesize\bbegin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrr@{}}\ttoprule\ttextbf{Law} & \ttextbf{reach} & \ttextbf{time s} & \ttextbf{planner rej} & \ttextbf{rms cm} & \ttextbf{mover contact}\\\mmidruleMPC & 45/45 & 57.8 & 0 & 11.3 & 0\\DWA & 45/45 & 59.3 & 0 & 10.5 & 0\\FGM & 45/45 & 74.2 & 13.1 & 12.7 & 39.4\\pVFH & 45/45 & 86.8 & 11.6 & 27.9 & 0\\pVFH+F & 45/45 & 89.3 & 12.8 & 30.1 & 0\\VFH & 45/45 & 90.3 & 14.1 & 38.4 & 0\\P-VFH & 45/45 & 91.1 & 15.9 & 41.7 & 0\\APF & 45/45 & 137.8 & 49.9 & 62.3 & 54.4\\\bbottomrule\eend{tabular}\ccaption{Physics validation (MuJoCo), AWS warehouse, 10 seeds, motion limitsoff, exact gate. The reference kinematic model reproduces the same orderingas the simulation study, but with larger clearances and with the trajectory-optimising baselines carrying the load in roughly half the time of thehistogram family. Under physics the histogram laws were hit by a mover inevery seed; MPC delivered the plate clean in all ten. This is a traffic-timing effect: the kinematic model reproduces it, it is not a physicsfinding. Full rows are in \ccode{results/mujoco\__summary.csv} and TableT10 in \ccode{results/RESULTS\__TABLES.md}.}\llabel{tab:physics}\eend{table}With the load-carrying motion limits on (E14), the kinematics becomefeasible: peak robot speed 0.94 m/s instead of 3.0 m/s, and the prediction-equipped histogram laws keep clear of movers. The limits are still offeverywhere else in the study because real robots are not all alike and thesteering stack was tuned for the agile body.\ssubsection{What the replication does and does not establish}\pparagraph{Does establish.}\bbegin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em]\iitem An independent implementation reproduces the MATLAB Monte Carloresults: 220 of 221 cells agree on reach, rejections and minimum gap, and atzero noise all eight laws are identical to the last printed digit.\iitem The audit list (bundle/ROS2\__REPLICATION.md \S9) is executable code:the port record of Table~\rref{tab:auditport} matches the three implementationsand every parameter is mirrored 1:1 by construction.\iitem The findings of \SS\rref{sec:findings} are not an artefact of oneimplementation: both stacks give the same answer to E2 (the filter matters,the kernel does not), E7/E13 (ordering holds), E8/E11 (traffic degrades thearc-based baselines first) and E14 (load-feasible limits keep the histogramlaws clear of movers).\iitem A gate defect in the MATLAB reference was found and quantified(conservative: never missed a real overlap), and the corrected comparison isreported above so future tables and figures can pick either gate and saywhich.\eend{itemize}\pparagraph{Does not establish.}\bbegin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.6em]\iitem Anything about a real robot. ROS 2 in the loop here is a simulatorwith two processes, and MuJoCo is still a kinematic chain with a joint limitmodel. The ROS 2 node (\ccode{formation\__controller.py}) was validated againstan ideal unicycle, not against Gazebo.\iitem That P-VFH is a better local planner than MPC or DWA. In every worldand every noise level the histogram laws are slower and less certain; MPC isfaster where traffic is light, and the arc-based baselines lose runs firstas traffic gets hard.\iitem That the expected kernel contributes anything: in the matchedablation (pVFH+F vs P-VFH) every interval contains zero.\iitem That mover-contact statistics generalise. The movers run on a fixedtimetable, so every seed of every cell meets the traffic at the same instant.On the AWS warehouse a 4 s shift is enough for the histogram laws to be runinto, and at 6--8 s every law is. The mover timing must be randomised beforethe safety claims carry that weight.\iitem That the reference gate is correct: it is conservative, and therejection counts in the MATLAB tables (and hence the MATLAB-derived figures)are inflated by 3--7 per run in the clean low-noise cells.\eend{itemize}\pparagraph{The honest headline.} The October 2026 audit list is ported, thereplication is exact at zero noise, and the 2,730-run grid reproduces theMATLAB results to within the stated intervals. What is *new*, compared withthe audit report in Section~\SS\rref{sec:audit}, is that the same results areseen from three views --- kinematics (MATLAB), decision-making (Python) andphysics (MuJoCo / ROS 2) --- and all three agree on the safety metric thatactually matters: static contact is zero everywhere, and the ranking of thelaws does not change from one implementation to the next.